# CS 255 System Analysis and Design Reflection

## Client and Project Summary

The client for this project was **DriverPass**, a company that wanted a system to help customers prepare for their driving exams. DriverPass needed a web-based platform that would allow students to access study materials, take practice tests, schedule driving lessons with instructors, and track their progress.

The system also needed to be secure, scalable, and user-friendly. It had to support reliable data storage and connect the online learning resources with the lesson scheduling features.

## Strengths in My Work

One area I did well in was capturing clear business requirements and translating them into system requirements. My documentation included both **functional requirements**, such as test scheduling and practice exams, and **non-functional requirements**, such as security and scalability.

This helped make sure the client’s expectations were clearly represented and that the system design supported the main goals of the project.

## Area I Would Revise

If I could revise one part of my work, I would improve the system diagrams. While I documented the requirements clearly, I could have created more detailed diagrams showing how data moves between different parts of the system.

Adding more detail to the diagrams would make it easier for developers and stakeholders to understand how the system works and how each part connects.

## Interpreting User Needs

The users of DriverPass needed a convenient and effective way to prepare for driving exams. I interpreted this need by designing a system that combined educational materials, practice tests, scheduling, and progress tracking in one platform.

Considering user needs is important because a system must solve the actual problems users face. Even if a system works technically, it may not be successful if it does not match what users need or expect.

## Approach to System Design

I approached the system design process by first reviewing the business requirements and then turning them into technical specifications. I identified the main system features, documented requirements, and created diagrams to show how users would interact with the system.

In the future, I would continue using this structured approach, but I would also include more iterative design techniques. For example, using Agile-style feedback and prototypes would help improve the design earlier in the process.

## Submission Notes

This README serves as my reflection on the **business requirements document** and **system design document** for the DriverPass project.

Both documents are included in this repository.
